From c81fc3728bb8ba79ef27ef337cb68938c8e13bac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marshall Lew <luqilinheu@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 10:06:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(desktop): give Linux a tray icon and a dock icon the
 panel can match

Two things a Linux desktop needs from this application, neither of which it
had.

The tray. Upstream wraps the whole DesktopTray construction in
`if (process.platform === 'win32')`, so on Linux no tray icon is ever
created. That interacts badly with the macOS-like behaviour this series
implements: closing the last window keeps the process and its Host running
by design, so a Linux user who closed the last window had a live application
with no way to reach it -- no dock icon either (see below), and the running
instance would swallow a second launch through the single-instance lock.
Windows gets the tray, macOS gets the Dock, Linux got nothing. Enable the
tray there too, and ship it a PNG: a StatusNotifier panel resolves the icon
at whatever size its theme asks for, and the multi-size Windows ICO is not
something libappindicator can consume.

The dock icon. StartupWMClass was left to electron-builder, which derives it
from productName -- "DeepSeek Harness" -- while the running window reports
WM_CLASS "deepseek-ai-dsh-desktop". Panels and docks match the two to decide
whether an icon belongs to a running window, so the mismatch made the dock
render a question mark for a window that was open and mapped. State the real
class.

patches/0004 notes that the tray icon is rendered the way
scripts/render-tray-icon.ts does for Windows; the same artwork is already
sized per-pixel in resources/icons, so the Linux tray reuses 32x32 from there.
